const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the Swa Pramanit Ghoshna Patra (self-declaration form)?', a: 'It is a signed self-declaration in which you state, in your own words and under your own signature, that the information given in your certificate application is true. Uttar Pradesh made it a standard document for income, caste and domicile certificates in place of the old notarized affidavit.' },
  { q: 'Why did the self-declaration replace the affidavit?', a: 'Earlier, applicants had to buy stamp paper and visit a notary for a sworn affidavit, which cost money and a full day of running around. The self-declaration reform removed that step: a plain signed declaration by the applicant carries the same responsibility, without stamp paper or notary charges.' },
  { q: 'Where do I download the self-declaration form?', a: 'The form is available free on the official eDistrict UP portal, edistrict.up.gov.in, and is also provided inside the eSathi application flow for the certificates that need it. Never pay a shop or agent for this form — it is a free one-page document.' },
  { q: 'In which format should I upload the self-declaration?', a: 'Upload it as a JPEG image only, kept under 100KB. For this particular document the portal rejects PDF files, which is a rule many applicants discover only after a rejection. Scan or photograph the signed form straight and in good light, then compress it below 100KB before uploading.' },
  { q: 'What is the most common reason applications get rejected because of this form?', a: 'A missing signature. An unsigned self-declaration is the single biggest cause of rejection in income, caste and domicile applications — the form carries no weight without the applicant\'s signature (and thumb impression where used). The second most common problem is uploading it as a PDF instead of a JPEG.' },
  { q: 'Can I write the declaration by hand instead of printing the form?', a: 'The safest practice is to print the standard form, fill it by hand in clear writing and sign it. If a printed form is genuinely not available, a neatly handwritten declaration containing the same details and your signature is generally what the verifying officer looks for — but use the printed format whenever possible to avoid a return.' },
  { q: 'Do I need a separate self-declaration for every certificate?', a: 'Yes. Each application — income, caste, domicile — is verified separately, so each one needs its own signed declaration mentioning that certificate and your details. Do not upload the same scanned copy in three applications; sign a fresh one for every application.' },
];
